Let's analyze the comment: "@@@ Each bit in the resulting logical length equals the bitwise or of its old value and the argument bit."

This means that after performing the `or` operation, each bit in the `BitSet` should be the result of a bitwise OR between its original value and the corresponding bit in the argument `BitSet`.

To express this in an assert statement, we need to compare the bits of the `BitSet` before and after the `or` operation with the bits of the argument `BitSet`. We can use the `get` method to access individual bits and `\old` to refer to the state before the method execution.

Here's the assert statement:

<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); this.get(i) == (\old(this.get(i)) || set.get(i));</code>

This asserts that for every index `i` within the size of the `BitSet`, the bit at position `i` in the `BitSet` after the `or` operation is equal to the bitwise OR of the bit at position `i` before the `or` operation and the bit at position `i` in the argument `BitSet`.
--------------------
<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.length(); this.get(i) == (\old(this.get(i)) || set.get(i));</code>
